Strength grows ~6–8 per token due to natural autoregressive self-similarity.

3. Novel Pattern Creation

If novel queries appear:

They occupy free attractor slots

No overwriting of active attractors

No unbounded memory growth

4. Decay & Eviction

Unused attractors passively decay each step.
If their strength falls below strength_min, they are fully evicted.

5. Burn-In (Critical Safety Mechanism)

DRAI V1 does not influence the model until memory is “mature.”

Two modes:

Strength-based burn-in (default)

Activate only after:

total_attractor_strength >= burn_in_threshold

Token-based burn-in (backup)

Activate after:

timestep >= burn_in_tokens


Prevents early-noise amplification and unstable self-feedback.

6. Synthetic Multi-Head K/V Generation

V1 emits:

k_reson, v_reson
Shape: [seq_len, batch, num_drai_heads, head_dim]


These vectors represent a global, normalized memory field, scaled via:

tanh(total_strength) * max_influence_scale


The vectors are produced by:

Weighted mean of alive attractors

Normalized and influence-bounded

Broadcast across DRAI heads

This replaces V0/V2’s unstable per-token attractor injection with a clean, interpretable field signal.

